---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const cin = [
  ['0x0', '1, 2 or 3', 'Miscellaneous function codes (reserved)'],
  ['0x1', '1, 2 or 3', 'Cable events (reserved)'],
  ['0x2', '2', 'Two-byte System Common: F1 (MTC quarter frame), F3 (Song Select)'],
  ['0x3', '3', 'Three-byte System Common: F2 (Song Position Pointer)'],
  ['0x4', '3', 'SysEx starts or continues'],
  ['0x5', '1', 'Single-byte System Common (F6), or SysEx ends with one byte (F7)'],
  ['0x6', '2', 'SysEx ends with the following two bytes'],
  ['0x7', '3', 'SysEx ends with the following three bytes'],
  ['0x8', '3', 'Note Off'],
  ['0x9', '3', 'Note On'],
  ['0xA', '3', 'Poly Key Pressure'],
  ['0xB', '3', 'Control Change'],
  ['0xC', '2', 'Program Change'],
  ['0xD', '2', 'Channel Pressure'],
  ['0xE', '3', 'Pitch Bend'],
  ['0xF', '1', 'Single byte (System Real Time, or unparsed bytes)'],
];
---

<Doc
  title="Transports"
  slug="transports"
  specs={[
    { label: 'MIDI specs', href: 'https://midi.org/specs' },
    { label: 'RFC 6295 (RTP-MIDI)', href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6295' },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    MIDI messages are the same on every wire. What changes is how bytes, or UMP packets, are framed, timed and
    powered. This page covers the five-pin cable, USB, Bluetooth LE and the network.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comparison">At a glance</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Transport</th><th>Raw rate</th><th>Typical latency</th><th>Direction</th><th>Carries</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5-pin DIN / TRS</td><td>31.25 kbit/s (~3,125 bytes/s)</td><td>~1 ms per 3-byte message, fixed and very low jitter</td><td>One way per cable</td><td>MIDI 1.0 bytes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB-MIDI 1.0</td><td>Full Speed USB, 12 Mbit/s bus</td><td>~1 ms polling interval, plus OS buffering</td><td>Bidirectional</td><td>MIDI 1.0 in 32-bit event packets, 16 cables</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB MIDI 2.0</td><td>Full or High Speed USB</td><td>Similar to USB 1.0; JR timestamps can smooth jitter</td><td>Bidirectional</td><td>UMP, 16 groups</td></tr>
        <tr><td>BLE-MIDI</td><td>Limited by the connection interval; a few kB/s in practice</td><td>~7.5–15 ms connection interval, with timestamps to reduce jitter</td><td>Bidirectional</td><td>MIDI 1.0 bytes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RTP-MIDI</td><td>Network speed</td><td>Sub-millisecond on wired LAN; variable on Wi-Fi</td><td>Bidirectional sessions</td><td>MIDI 1.0 bytes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Network MIDI 2.0</td><td>Network speed</td><td>Similar to RTP-MIDI</td><td>Bidirectional sessions</td><td>UMP</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="din">5-pin DIN</h2>
  <p>
    The original MIDI 1.0 transport is still common on synths, drum machines and modular gear. It is an
    <strong>asynchronous serial</strong> link: 1 start bit, 8 data bits (LSB first), no parity and 1 stop bit, at
    <strong>31,250 bit/s ±1%</strong>. Each byte takes 10 bit-times, or <strong>320 µs</strong>. A 3-byte Note On
    takes 960 µs, so a ten-note chord is spread over roughly 10 ms. That is one reason for
    <a href="/docs/messages/#running-status">running status</a>, which trims it to about 6.7 ms.
  </p>
  <h3 id="din-electrical">Current loop and opto-isolation</h3>
  <p>
    The signal is a <strong>~5 mA current loop</strong>, not a voltage. The transmitter sources current on pin 4 and
    sinks it on pin 5. At the receiver, that current drives the LED of an <strong>opto-isolator</strong>, so the two
    devices' grounds never connect. This is why MIDI rarely causes ground-loop hum.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pin</th><th>MIDI OUT / THRU</th><th>MIDI IN</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>Current source, through R<sub>A</sub> to the supply</td><td>To opto-isolator LED anode, through the input resistor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>Current sink, through R<sub>C</sub> to the UART TX (driven low for a 0 bit)</td><td>To opto-isolator LED cathode</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>Shield, connected to ground</td><td><strong>Not connected</strong>, to avoid ground loops</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1, 3</td><td>Not used</td><td>Not used</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The <strong>2014 MIDI 1.0 Electrical Specification Update</strong> added 3.3 V transmitters and adjusted
    resistor values:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Transmitter supply</th><th>R<sub>A</sub> (pin 4)</th><th>R<sub>C</sub> (pin 5)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>+5 V</td><td>220 Ω</td><td>220 Ω</td></tr>
        <tr><td>+3.3 V</td><td>33 Ω</td><td>10 Ω</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The receiver uses a fast opto-isolator such as the 6N138 or H11L1, with a series resistor (220 Ω is typical)
    and a reverse-protection diode across the LED. MIDI THRU should copy the IN signal electrically. A long chain of
    THRUs gradually distorts the bit timing, so long chains are best split with a THRU box.
  </p>
  <Callout type="warn" title="Wiring mistakes">
    <p>
      Pin numbers on a DIN connector are not in physical order. Around the arc, the pins run 1, 4, 2, 5, 3, and
      the view from the solder side is the mirror image of the front. Check the spec drawing before soldering.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="trs">TRS (3.5 mm) MIDI</h2>
  <p>
    Compact devices often use 3.5 mm TRS jacks with a breakout adapter. MMA document <strong>CA-033</strong> (2018)
    standardized the wiring as <strong>Type A</strong>. An older convention, <strong>Type B</strong>, swaps tip and
    ring and is still found on some devices. Plugging a Type B device into a Type A adapter usually doesn't damage
    anything; it just doesn't work.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Contact</th><th>Type A (standard)</th><th>Type B</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tip</td><td>Pin 5 (current sink)</td><td>Pin 4 (current source)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ring</td><td>Pin 4 (current source)</td><td>Pin 5 (current sink)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sleeve</td><td>Pin 2 (shield)</td><td>Pin 2 (shield)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="usb">USB-MIDI 1.0</h2>
  <p>
    The <em>USB Device Class Definition for MIDI Devices</em> (v1.0, 1999) lets class-compliant devices work without
    vendor drivers on macOS, Windows, Linux, iOS and Android. MIDI data travels over bulk endpoints in
    <strong>32-bit USB-MIDI Event Packets</strong>:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Byte 0</th><th>Byte 1</th><th>Byte 2</th><th>Byte 3</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cable Number (high nibble, 0–15) | Code Index Number (low nibble)</td><td>MIDI byte 0</td><td>MIDI byte 1</td><td>MIDI byte 2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The <strong>Cable Number</strong> picks one of up to 16 virtual cables (ports) on the endpoint. The
    <strong>Code Index Number (CIN)</strong> classifies the message so the host knows how many of the three bytes
    are valid. Unused bytes are padded with 0. Note On, channel 1, middle C, velocity 100 on cable 0 is
    <code>09 90 3C 64</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>CIN</th><th class="num">MIDI bytes</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {cin.map(([c, n, m]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{c}</code></td><td class="num">{n}</td><td>{m}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    SysEx is split into 3-byte chunks with CIN 0x4, and the last chunk uses CIN 0x5, 0x6 or 0x7 depending on how
    many bytes remain, including the closing F7. Running status is not used on USB; every packet carries its full
    status byte.
  </p>

  <h2 id="usb2">USB MIDI 2.0</h2>
  <p>
    The USB MIDI 2.0 device class (2020) keeps backward compatibility through USB alternate settings:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alternate Setting 0</strong> is a normal USB-MIDI 1.0 interface, so older hosts keep working.</li>
    <li><strong>Alternate Setting 1</strong> carries <a href="/docs/midi-2/#ump">Universal MIDI Packets</a> directly over the bulk endpoints.</li>
    <li><strong>Group Terminal Blocks</strong> in the descriptors say which UMP groups exist and whether they are inputs, outputs or both. At runtime, UMP Function Blocks give the richer picture.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Hosts with MIDI 2.0 drivers (macOS 11+, Linux 6.5+, Android 13+, Windows MIDI Services) select Alternate
    Setting 1. Other hosts use Alternate Setting 0. On microcontrollers, TinyUSB has MIDI 1.0 device support, and
    UMP drivers such as <a href="https://github.com/midi2-dev/tusb_ump">tusb_ump</a> add MIDI 2.0.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ble">BLE-MIDI</h2>
  <p>
    <em>MIDI over Bluetooth Low Energy</em> (2015) is built into macOS, iOS, Android 6+ and, more recently, Windows.
    A device exposes one GATT service with one characteristic. The characteristic supports read (which returns an
    empty payload), write without response, and notify.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>UUID</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>MIDI Service</td><td><code>03B80E5A-EDE8-4B33-A751-6CE34EC4C700</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>MIDI I/O Characteristic</td><td><code>7772E5DB-3868-4112-A1A9-F2669D106BF3</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3 id="ble-packet">Packet format</h3>
  <p>Each BLE packet carries one or more MIDI messages with a 13-bit millisecond timestamp:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Header byte</strong>: bit 7 = 1, bit 6 = 0, bits 5–0 = the upper 6 bits of the timestamp.</li>
    <li><strong>Timestamp byte</strong>: bit 7 = 1, bits 6–0 = the lower 7 bits of the timestamp. One comes before each MIDI message in the packet. Because its top bit is set, it can't be mistaken for a data byte.</li>
    <li>Then the MIDI message itself. Running status is allowed within a packet, and SysEx can span several packets.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>80         header: timestamp high bits = 0
81         timestamp low bits = 1   → t = 1 ms
90 3C 64   Note On C4, velocity 100
81         timestamp (t = 1 ms)
3E 64      Note On D4, velocity 100 (running status)</code></pre>
  <p>
    The timestamp wraps every 8,192 ms. Receivers use it to place events correctly in time, because BLE delivers
    data in bursts once per <em>connection interval</em>. The spec recommends an interval of 15 ms or less, and 7.5 ms
    is the minimum BLE allows. Expect more latency and jitter than over a cable, and choose USB or DIN when timing is
    critical.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rtp">RTP-MIDI (AppleMIDI)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>RTP-MIDI</strong> (RFC 6295, which replaced RFC 4695) carries MIDI over IP in RTP packets. A
    <strong>recovery journal</strong> lets a receiver rebuild the important state (notes, controllers, program) after
    a lost packet without retransmission. Apple's <strong>AppleMIDI</strong> session protocol, built into macOS and
    iOS as "Network MIDI", is the de facto way to set up sessions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each session uses two consecutive UDP ports: a <strong>control</strong> port <var>N</var> (even) and a <strong>data</strong> port <var>N</var>+1. 5004/5005 is a common default.</li>
    <li>Session commands are invitation (<code>IN</code>), accept (<code>OK</code>), reject (<code>NO</code>), end (<code>BY</code>), clock synchronization (<code>CK</code>) and receiver feedback (<code>RS</code>).</li>
    <li>Sessions are advertised over Bonjour/mDNS as <code>_apple-midi._udp</code>.</li>
    <li>On Windows, use Tobias Erichsen's rtpMIDI driver. On Linux, <a href="https://github.com/davidmoreno/rtpmidid">rtpmidid</a>. On microcontrollers, the <a href="https://github.com/lathoub/Arduino-AppleMIDI-Library">Arduino AppleMIDI library</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="network-midi-2">Network MIDI 2.0 (UDP)</h2>
  <p>
    Published by the MMA and AMEI in 2024, <strong>Network MIDI 2.0</strong> sends
    <a href="/docs/midi-2/#ump">UMP</a> over UDP. It defines mDNS discovery, session setup with optional
    authentication, and retransmission of recent packets to recover from loss. It is to MIDI 2.0 what RTP-MIDI is to
    MIDI 1.0, and is a natural fit for UMP-native stacks such as Windows MIDI Services.
  </p>

  <h3 id="ipmidi">ipMIDI and other approaches</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>ipMIDI</strong> sends raw MIDI bytes in UDP <em>multicast</em> packets (group 225.0.0.37, one port per
    MIDI port starting at 21928). It has no sessions and no recovery, but it is simple and is supported by tools such
    as <a href="https://github.com/rncbc/qmidinet">QmidiNet</a>. For tempo sync rather than MIDI messages, many
    applications use <a href="https://github.com/Ableton/link">Ableton Link</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout title="Choosing a transport">
    <ul>
      <li>Tightest timing between hardware: <strong>DIN</strong> or <strong>USB</strong> with a good interface.</li>
      <li>Computer to device, plug-and-play: <strong>USB</strong>, with USB MIDI 2.0 if both ends support it.</li>
      <li>Wireless controllers on stage or iPad setups: <strong>BLE-MIDI</strong>. Accept a few milliseconds of latency.</li>
      <li>Between computers or across a studio: <strong>RTP-MIDI</strong> or <strong>Network MIDI 2.0</strong> on wired Ethernet.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Doc>
